title Kiln takes the craft seat, and a granted fold turns out to be a compiler change
prompt Keaton: approve and kg and improve the whole repo.
think The approval freed the craft faculty to Kiln, and that closes the part-whole collision cleanly rather than merely naming it. Ember rose to the vane and gathered a member of its own name; Kiln had taken the bake seat on 20260827.025117, a day earlier and by the same hand, and had been standing free since. So the repair was already sitting in the tree and only needed a word. Ember is the heat KEPT and a kiln is the chamber where that heat is APPLIED -- the two names stay true to each other, and Ember's senses drop from four to three with none containing another.
think The molt took the M1 shape because the elder is a dated foundation page with a name that had become wrong: foundations/20260823-212606_the-ember-voice.md could not keep a filename saying ember-voice once the voice was Kiln. A mutant at a fresh stamp, the elder bannered with its reason, seven living citers repointed, and a Class M row in SHRED_PREP with the cut left RED.
think The kg lap was the glow/gen letter fold, granted earlier, and it stopped being a kg lap when I read the code. glow/glow_run.rye builds the /+ library import path as the flat format string glow/gen/{s}.glow at its lines 166 and 241, so folding the room by first letter breaks import resolution for the 31 desks that carry a /+ line. A granted door is not the same as a mechanical door, and the honest move was to say so rather than to start moving 352 files.
think So the lap turned to the defect underneath it, which was the more valuable half anyway. The room was 340 against a 256 bound with every meter green, and I went to read WHY rather than trusting that a green meter had looked.
obs The meter was blind by construction, and not for one room. tools/fixtures/room_bound_scan.sh discovers rooms by walking for DATED basenames, so a directory whose files are named for their subject reads zero forever and never arrives at the meter at all. An undated sweep that asks git ls-files instead found FIVE rooms over the bound on its first run -- tools/fixtures 649, tools/gen/chapter 434, glow/gen 339, counsel/date/20260728 311, active-designing/yonder 296 -- with enforced_over=0 and advised_over=0 printed beside them. The largest is 2.5x over. The tools entry on ENFORCE_ALL counts that directory's own flat entries and reaches none of its subdirectories, which is where two of the five sit.
obs The mark law already carries the sentence that covers this -- a room that vanishes from a meter is not a room that passed it -- and it reaches one step further than it was written: a room that never ARRIVES at a meter has not passed it either. Booked as REDS %301, with the five folds open as five laps rather than one sweep, and the sweep left advisory on purpose because a fold is not always mechanical.
obs Three living pins hit their bound in one day. REDS folded twice more this lap (%298 earlier, %299 here) and SHRED_PREP needed six trims to fit a four-line Class M row. A pin that can only accept work after a fold is a pin whose fold cadence is now part of the round rather than an exception to it.
loom reds_booked=301 reds_folded=298,299 rooms_over_found=5 largest_room=649 bound=256 kiln_citers_repointed=7 senses_now=3 pins_at_bound=3
file foundations/20260827-195316_the-kiln-voice.md the craft voice under its own name, in Twilight
file foundations/20260823-212606_the-ember-voice.md the elder, bannered, every word kept
file context/LEXICON.md the Ember row drops to three senses; the Kiln row carries two seats that are one image
file tools/fixtures/room_bound_scan.sh the undated sweep -- ask git for every directory rather than the tree for the rooms it knows
file construction/REDS.md %301, the five rooms nobody named
file construction/SHRED_PREP.md the Class M row for the elder page, cut RED
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- five room folds are booked and each is its own lap; glow/gen's is a compiler change to the /+ path in glow_run.rye rather than a rename, and FORA31 and the deep debride still stand behind them
